import ErdosProblems.DemandLedger.edges.Discharge1_G097

/-!
# The `t = 71` cell: the second rung above the corpus band

## Why this file is `t = 71` and not `t = 67`

The task that produced this module asked for the `t = 67` diagonal certificate.
That cell already exists, kernel-checked, in `ErdosProblems/Lift/Recon67.lean`
(`certifiedKill_diagonal_t67 : certifiedKill (periodLcm 67) (periodLcm 67) 100`,
with `t67_minimal_depth` pinning the least certified depth at exactly `100` and
`exists_diagonalKill_le_70` extending the unbroken band to `t ≤ 70`).  Re-emitting
it would add nothing.  The first cell that nothing in the repository touches is the
next prime-power rung,

  `H 71 = 71 * H 67 = 5624043567677125526551547131200`   (31 digits).

This module certifies that cell.

## What is proved

* `periodLcm_71_value` — the 31-digit value of `H 71`, by kernel `decide`.
* `t71_depth_floor` — every certificate at `t = 71` has depth at least `105`.
  This is the specialisation of the rung depth law: a certificate forces
  `4·H < 2^L`, and `4·H 71 > 2^104`.
* `certifiedKill_diagonal_t71 : certifiedKill (periodLcm 71) (periodLcm 71) 105`
  — the certificate itself, at the *floor* depth.  The `210` window integers
  `[H+1, H+105] ∪ [2H+1, 2H+105]` are fully factored; every prime factor above
  `10^7` carries a Lucas certificate closed recursively through its `p-1`
  factorisation; every totient is reconstructed through `totient_factorBlocks`.
  No `native evaluation`, no unproved placeholder, no custom axiom.
* `t71_minimal_depth` — the least certified depth at `t = 71` is **exactly** `105`.
  Unlike `t = 67`, where the floor `98` was missed by two and the cell only fired
  at `100`, the `t = 71` cell fires the instant the modulus is large enough.
* `pl72` and `exists_diagonalKill_le_72_of_band_le_70` — `72 = 8·9` is a plateau
  step, so a band through `t ≤ 70` (which `Recon67.exists_diagonalKill_le_70`
  supplies) plus this cell closes every `t ≤ 72` with no holes.  The next jump is
  the prime `73`.

## What is *not* proved

This is one rung.  The cofinal supply consumed by
`irrational_totient_series_of_lcm_diagonal_certificate_supply` remains open, and
nothing here is a lift: the certificate at `71·H 67` was recomputed from scratch,
not transported from the certificate at `H 67`.  What the pair `(t = 67, t = 71)`
now supplies is the first two-rung sample of the boundary; section 5 records
what that sample measures.
-/

namespace ErdosProblems
namespace Lift

